Abstract

Human civilization is transitioning from a single‑planet species to a distributed multiplanetary one. Existing theories of political science, economics, and governance were developed under assumptions of near‑instantaneous communication, a shared planetary environment, and centralized institutional oversight. None of these assumptions hold when populations are distributed across Earth, the Moon, Mars, and beyond, where communication delays range from seconds to tens of minutes. This dissertation proposes the discipline of Multiplanetary Civilization Architecture (MCA) : the science of designing, modeling, simulating, and governing civilizations whose components are separated by planetary‑scale distances, ecological incommensurability, autonomous AI infrastructures, and significant time lags.

The central hypothesis is that a civilization can be represented as a multilayer adaptive network whose long‑term viability depends on maintaining coherence across six coupled domains: Physical Infrastructure (P), Ecological Systems (E), Governance (G), Knowledge (K), Autonomous AI (A), and the Noösphere (N)—the aggregate cognitive and spiritual state. A sixth layer, the Noösphere, is introduced as a vital coupling field. A mathematical framework is developed using graph theory, control theory, delay differential equations, category theory, information theory, and dynamical systems to define conditions under which a civilization remains stable, adaptive, and resilient.

The core research question is: How can a civilization remain coherent when its members no longer inhabit the same world?

Key contributions include: a formalisation of civilisational coherence CC(t) using normalised mutual information and pairwise compatibility functions; a proof that delays induce exponential coherence decay without active control; a delay‑tolerant governance theory with an information‑theoretic control‑rate bound (the civilisational analogue of the data‑rate theorem) and Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability conditions; a geometric reformulation of constitutional law as sections of a fiber bundle with delayed parallel transport, where legal fragmentation is curvature and the obstruction to global coherence is measured by sheaf cohomology H^1; a Landau‑style phase transition model for the AI‑Human agency ratio R(t), demonstrating that large delays force a separation into human‑reserve and AI‑dominant domains; a Kuramoto synchronisation model of the Noösphere with a critical coupling threshold \lambda_c(\tau); and the construction of a Civilisational Digital Twin (CDT) as a relativistic Cauchy surface and distributed observer, with a Landauer–Shannon energy bound.

The dissertation concludes by defining the profession of the Multiplanetary Civilization Architect, whose mandate is to design the coupling topology, stabilise coherence, manage agency phase boundaries, nurture the Noösphere, and maintain the CDT—ensuring that humanity’s expansion does not become its speciation.

The Moon and Mars are not merely engineering targets; they are constitutional design spaces. This work provides the first formal blueprints.


Chapter 1: Introduction — The End of Simultaneity

1.1 The Historical Accident of Instantaneity

All recorded theories of governance, economics, and civilisational design rest on an unexamined axiom: simultaneity. From the Athenian agora to the United Nations, political coordination has assumed that information and authority can propagate across the polity faster than the events they govern. The speed of light was, for all practical purposes, infinite.

The Roman Empire did not fall merely because its messages took months; it fell in part because its control loop—sense, decide, act—was chronically open-loop. The British Empire strained under the six‑week lag of steamship telegraphy. Yet even these were treated as frictional inefficiencies, never as structural constants. The governing assumption was always that eventually the centre could hear the periphery, and the delay could be absorbed into diplomacy.

The 21st‑century spacefaring transition breaks this axiom permanently. Light‑speed becomes a hard physical ceiling.

· Earth–Moon: \tau \approx 1.3\text{ s} — voice‑lag, tolerable but already non‑negligible for high‑frequency trading or automated conflict resolution.
· Earth–Mars: \tau \in [3, 22]\text{ min} — no shared OODA loop, no real‑time dialogue.
· Earth–Jovian system: \tau > 30\text{ min} — governance becomes asynchronous by physical law.

Definition 1.1.1 (The End of Simultaneity).
A multiplaletary civilisation is any human or post‑human collective whose spatial extent implies \tau_{ij} > T_{\text{event}} for key governance and crisis‑response events, where T_{\text{event}} is the characteristic timescale of the event. When \tau > T_{\text{event}}, institutions predicated on synchronous consensus—parliaments, real‑time markets, centralised command—become mathematically unstable without explicit delay‑compensating controllers.

This is not a communications engineering problem. It is a civilisational architecture problem.

---

1.2 The Breakdown of Three Classical Assumptions

Classical political science, systems engineering, and economics fail under multiplanetary conditions because three axioms dissolve:

1. Shared Clock Assumption. All agents can agree on “now.” Relativity plus finite \tau destroys this. There exists no universal Cauchy surface for the civilisation. My “present” is your “past light cone.” Any synchronised decision protocol therefore requires a constructed global state—a digital twin built from asynchronous data.
2. Centralised Oversight Assumption. The centre can observe, decide, and command. With \tau > 0, any centralised command is controlling a system state that is \tau seconds old. Control theory calls this a delayed feedback system. Uncompensated, such systems oscillate or diverge; the phase margin shrinks with delay.
3. Homogeneous Environment Assumption. All citizens share one biosphere, one set of physical constraints. Multiplanetary settlements have incommensurable survival functions: lunar vacuum, Martian perchlorates, microgravity physiology. Law, economics, and ethics cannot be uniform; they must be sheaf‑like—locally valid, globally compatible only under specific gluing conditions.

Therefore, the nation‑state, the corporation, the federation—all are \tau \to 0 special cases of a broader object.

