1.4 The Core Thesis: Coherence as a Controllable Observable

We reject the notion that “culture” or “shared values” alone can hold a multi‑world civilisation together. They are necessary but insufficient when \tau > T_{\text{event}}. Civilisational viability must be reduced to a measurable, controllable scalar.

Thesis 1.4.1 (The Coherence Thesis).
A multiplanetary civilisation \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{M} is viable in the long term iff there exists a control policy \pi acting on the coupling functions C_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)} and on the constitutional trajectory \Theta(t) such that

CC(t) = \frac{ \sum_{i,j} w_{ij}\, S_{ij}\big(\Theta_i(t), \Theta_j(t-\tau_{ij})\big) }{ \sum_{i,j} w_{ij} }

is maintained above a critical threshold \theta_c for all t, despite exogenous shocks and irreducible interplanetary delays. The pairwise compatibilities S_{ij} are defined in Chapter 2 using a combination of legal distance, economic correlation, and mutual information; they satisfy a data processing inequality (Theorem 2.4.3) that limits how coherence can be routed through intermediaries.

