2.1 The Multilayer Network Formalism

A multiplanetary civilisation is a distributed system of settlements and infrastructures linked by physical transport, communication channels, shared knowledge, and collective narratives. To capture this complexity without losing mathematical tractability, we model the civilisation as a multilayer network using the formalism of Boccaletti et al. (2014) and the tensorial approach of Bianconi (2018).

Definition 2.1.1 (Civilisational Multilayer Network).
Let the civilisation comprise N distinct physical settlements. It is represented by a multilayer network \mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{L}, \mathcal{E}, w) where:

· \mathcal{V} is the set of node‑layer pairs (i, \alpha), with i \in \{1,\dots,N\} and \alpha \in \mathcal{L};
· \mathcal{L} = \{P, E, G, K, A, N\} is the set of six layers: Physical, Ecological, Governance, Knowledge, Autonomous AI, and Noösphere;
· \mathcal{E} is the edge set, partitioned into intra‑layer edges \mathcal{E}_{\text{intra}} (connecting the same layer across different settlements) and inter‑layer edges \mathcal{E}_{\text{inter}} (connecting different layers, either at the same settlement or across settlements);
· w: \mathcal{E} \to \mathbb{R}^+ assigns a coupling strength to each edge.

Definition 2.1.2 (Supra‑Adjacency and Supra‑Laplacian).
The global topology is encoded in the supra‑adjacency matrix \mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{NL \times NL}, whose block structure places the intra‑layer adjacency matrices \mathbf{A}^{[\alpha]} on the diagonal and the inter‑layer coupling matrices \mathbf{C}^{[\alpha\beta]} off‑diagonally. The corresponding supra‑Laplacian is

\mathbf{L} = \bigoplus_{\alpha=1}^{L} \mathbf{L}^{[\alpha]} + \omega \sum_{\alpha \neq \beta} \mathbf{D}^{[\alpha\beta]},
\tag{2.1}

where \mathbf{L}^{[\alpha]} = \mathbf{D}^{[\alpha]} - \mathbf{A}^{[\alpha]} is the combinatorial Laplacian of layer \alpha, \mathbf{D}^{[\alpha\beta]} encodes the inter‑layer diffusion, and \omega controls the global coupling intensity. The spectral properties of \mathbf{L}—in particular its algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(\mathbf{L}) and spectral gap—govern the relaxation time, the onset of synchronisation, and the stability of coherent states under perturbations (Theorem 1.1, Background).

Definition 2.1.3 (Civilisation State Tensor).
For each node‑layer pair (i,\alpha) we associate a state vector x_\alpha^{(i)}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\alpha}. The full state is conveniently collected in the civilisation state tensor \mathcal{X}_{i\alpha}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times L \times d_{\max}}. For notational simplicity, we often work with the vectorised form \mathbf{X}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{D}, D = N\sum_\alpha d_\alpha, but the tensorial structure is essential for decoupling the dynamics.

The dimensions d_\alpha and interpretations are:

· Physical (d_P=4): available power, structural integrity, fleet capacity, backup latency.
· Ecological (d_E=5): O₂, CO₂, water reserves, biodiversity, soil toxicity.
· Governance (d_G=3): autonomy parameter \theta_i \in [0,1], institutional trust, emergency threshold.
· Knowledge (d_K=2): total stored information (bits), discovery rate (bits/year).
· AI (d_A=2): AI agency A_i(t) (equivalent decision‑capacity), alignment score.
· Noösphere (d_N=1): narrative cohesion index n_i(t) \in [-1,1].

Axiom 2.1.4 (Locality of Dynamics).
Each node‑layer’s state evolves under its own local dynamics and is influenced by other node‑layer pairs through coupling functions that respect light‑speed delays.

