2.2 Coupled Dynamics with Interplanetary Delay

The evolution of the civilisation is governed by a system of delay differential equations (DDEs):

\boxed{
\frac{d}{dt} x_\alpha^{(i)}(t) = G_\alpha^{(i)}\big(x_\alpha^{(i)}(t)\big)
+ \sum_{\beta} \sum_{j \neq i} C_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)}\big( x_\alpha^{(i)}(t),\, x_\beta^{(j)}(t - \tau_{ij}) \big)
+ \Xi_\alpha^{(i)}(t).
}
\tag{2.2}

Here:

· G_\alpha^{(i)} are the intrinsic local dynamics (ecological succession, resource consumption, legislative drift).
· C_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)} is the coupling function from layer \beta at node j to layer \alpha at node i, evaluated with the delay \tau_{ij} = d_{ij}/c.
· \Xi_\alpha^{(i)} are bounded stochastic shocks.

Theorem 2.2.1 (Existence and Uniqueness of Civilisation Trajectories).
Assume that each G_\alpha^{(i)} and C_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)} is locally Lipschitz continuous, the coupling functions are bounded on compact sets, and the state space is compact. Then for any initial history segment \mathbf{X}_0(\cdot) \in C([-\tau_{\max},0], \mathbb{R}^D), there exists a unique global solution \mathbf{X}(t) to (2.2) for all t \geq 0.

Proof. This is a direct consequence of the Picard–Lindelöf theorem extended to DDEs (Hale & Verduyn Lunel, 1993). Compactness prevents finite‑time blow‑up, guaranteeing global existence.

---

2.3 The Linearised Consensus Protocol and the Master Stability Function

To build intuition, we isolate the Governance layer and linearise the couplings around a consensus state. Assume each settlement has a scalar legislative alignment variable \theta_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}, with no intrinsic drift (G_G^{(i)} = 0), and that the coupling from j to i is linear:

C_{GG}^{(ij)}(\theta_i(t), \theta_j(t-\tau_{ij})) = a_{ij}\big( \theta_j(t-\tau_{ij}) - \theta_i(t) \big),

where a_{ij} \ge 0 and the coupling graph is strongly connected. This yields the delayed linear consensus protocol:

\frac{d\theta_i}{dt} = \sum_{j=1}^N a_{ij}\big( \theta_j(t-\tau_{ij}) - \theta_i(t) \big), \quad i=1,\dots,N.
\tag{2.3}

To analyse stability, we vectorise: \Theta(t) = [\theta_1,\dots,\theta_N]^\top. The system can be written using the delay‑structured Laplacian \mathcal{L}_\tau, but a more powerful decomposition is obtained via the generalised master stability function (MSF) formalism.

Theorem 2.3.1 (MSF for Delayed Consensus).
Let \mathbf{L}_0 be the zero‑delay Laplacian of the graph with weights a_{ij}, and let \lambda_2,\dots,\lambda_N be its nonzero eigenvalues. The synchronised manifold \theta_1 = \dots = \theta_N is locally exponentially stable if and only if, for each i=2,\dots,N, the scalar delayed differential equation

\dot{y}(t) = -\lambda_i\, y(t) + \lambda_i\, y(t-\tau)
\tag{2.4}

is asymptotically stable, where \tau is a representative delay (for identical delays). For heterogeneous delays \tau_{ij}, a similar decoupling holds using the eigenvectors of the Laplacian (Background, Theorem 12.1).

Proof. Let \Theta(t) = \mathbf{1}\bar{\theta}(t) + \delta\Theta(t), where \mathbf{1} is the consensus direction. Decompose \delta\Theta in the eigenbasis of \mathbf{L}_0: \delta\Theta(t) = \sum_{i=2}^N \eta_i(t)\mathbf{v}_i. Projecting (2.3) yields the decoupled modes (2.4) with \lambda_i replacing the effective coupling. Stability of the full system is equivalent to simultaneous stability of all transverse modes. ∎

The stability of (2.4) is well understood: for a given \lambda_i, there exists a maximal delay \tau_{\max}(\lambda_i) beyond which the mode becomes unstable. The smallest such \tau_{\max} over all i determines the stability margin of the governance layer. For Earth–Mars delays, even modest coupling strengths push the system across this threshold, necessitating active control (Chapter 3).

