2.4 Defining Civilisational Coherence CC(t)

To make Thesis 1.4.1 operational, we require a scalar metric that aggregates pairwise compatibilities across all settlements and layers. The Background document (Section 3) provides the rigorous building blocks.

Definition 2.4.1 (Pairwise Compatibility via Information Theory).
For any two settlements i and j, and any pair of layers \alpha, \beta, the layer‑wise compatibility is

S_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)}(t) = \frac{I(X_\alpha^{(i)}(t); X_\beta^{(j)}(t))}{\min\{H(X_\alpha^{(i)}), H(X_\beta^{(j)})\}},

where I(\cdot;\cdot) is mutual information and H(\cdot) is Shannon entropy. This normalised mutual information lies in [0,1] and captures the degree to which the state of one settlement‑layer predicts the other, after accounting for the delay implicitly through the time‑lagged dependencies.

For practical analysis, we also employ more specific forms:

· Legal compatibility (G–G): S_{GG}^{(ij)} = \exp\!\big(-\tau_{ij}/\tau_G\big) \cdot \exp\!\big(-\|\theta_i - \theta_j\|^2/2\sigma_\theta^2\big).
· Economic compatibility (P–P): correlation of resource stock vectors, with a delay penalty.
· Noöspheric compatibility (N–N): normalised mutual information between narrative embedding vectors.

Definition 2.4.2 (Total Pairwise Compatibility).
The total compatibility between settlements i and j is the weighted sum over relevant layer pairs:

S_{ij}(t) = \frac{ \sum_{\alpha,\beta} v_{\alpha\beta} \, S_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)}(t) }{ \sum_{\alpha,\beta} v_{\alpha\beta} },

with weights v_{\alpha\beta} reflecting the relative importance of cross‑layer alignment.

Definition 2.4.3 (Civilisational Coherence).
The coherence of the civilisation at time t is

\boxed{
CC(t) = \frac{ \sum_{i,j=1}^N w_{ij} \, S_{ij}(t) }{ \sum_{i,j=1}^N w_{ij} },
}
\tag{2.5}

where w_{ij} = \sqrt{\text{pop}_i \cdot \text{pop}_j} (or another importance weight). By construction, CC(t) \in [0,1]. A value of 1 corresponds to perfect alignment across all layers and settlements; 0 to total fragmentation.

Theorem 2.4.4 (Coherence Bounds).
For any multilayer civilisation, 0 \le CC(t) \le 1, with CC=1 iff all settlements are perfectly synchronised across all domains, and CC=0 iff all settlements are completely decoupled (mutual information zero for all pairs).

Proof. Each S_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)} \in [0,1]; the weighted average preserves the bounds. The extremal conditions follow from the properties of normalised mutual information. ∎

Theorem 2.4.5 (Data Processing Inequality for Coherence).
If settlement k acts as an information intermediary between i and j (i.e., i \leftrightarrow k \leftrightarrow j forms a Markov chain), then

S_{ij}(t) \le \min\{S_{ik}(t), S_{kj}(t)\}.

Proof. This is a direct consequence of the data processing inequality: I(X_i; X_j) \le \min\{I(X_i; X_k), I(X_k; X_j)\}. Normalising by the minimum entropy preserves the inequality. ∎

This inequality has profound implications: coherence cannot be improved by routing through an intermediate settlement; it can only be degraded. Intermediaries can act as bottlenecks, and the architect must design the coupling graph to ensure that critical pairs share a direct, high‑capacity link.

