2.5 Exponential Decay Without Control: The MSF Proof

We now prove the central necessity result: an uncontrolled multiplanetary civilisation with delays inevitably decoheres. The argument uses the MSF decomposition of Section 2.3 and the information‑theoretic coherence definition.

Theorem 2.5.1 (Delay‑Induced Coherence Drift).
Consider a civilisation whose Governance layer evolves under the linear delayed consensus protocol (2.3) on a strongly connected graph, and assume at least one pair (i,j) has \tau_{ij} > 0. Then there exists a critical delay \tau^* (the smallest delay that destabilises any transverse mode) such that for \tau > \tau^*, the synchronised state is unstable, and the governance pairwise compatibility S_{GG}^{(ij)}(t) decays exponentially. Consequently, the overall coherence CC(t) satisfies

CC(t) \le C_0 e^{-\lambda_d t} \quad \text{for large } t,

where \lambda_d = \min_{i \ge 2} \Re(s_i) > 0 is the least stable eigenvalue of the delayed Laplacian.

Proof. The MSF Theorem 2.3.1 decouples the system into independent scalar modes (2.4). For each mode, the characteristic equation is s + \lambda_i - \lambda_i e^{-s\tau} = 0. For \tau = 0, all modes have s = -\lambda_i < 0 (stable). As \tau increases, a pair of eigenvalues crosses the imaginary axis at \tau_i^* = \frac{\pi}{2\lambda_i} (for undirected, uniform delay). Let \tau^* = \min_i \tau_i^* be the first instability. For \tau > \tau^*, at least one mode has \Re(s) > 0, so \|\delta\Theta(t)\| grows exponentially. Since S_{GG}^{(ij)} decays with \|\theta_i - \theta_j\|, it inherits this exponential decay. As the governance layer couples into all other layers via the supra‑Laplacian, the overall coherence CC(t) cannot remain above threshold. ∎

Corollary 2.5.2. The interlayer coherence phase transition (Background, Theorem 1.2) provides a sharper condition: if the mean intra‑layer degree is \langle k \rangle and inter‑layer edges are present with probability p, the critical threshold p_c^{\pm} below which layers decouple is

p_c^{\pm} \approx \frac{1}{2}\left(1 \pm \frac{1}{2\langle k \rangle}\right).

Thus, not only delay but also insufficient inter‑layer coupling can trigger a fragmentation cascade.

