3.4 Lyapunov–Krasovskii Functionals and LMI Conditions

Frequency‑domain methods become unwieldy for nonlinear couplings and heterogeneous delays. A more general tool is the Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional (LKF) approach, which yields Linear Matrix Inequality (LMI) conditions that can be solved computationally. The Background document provides a foundational theorem (Theorem 4.3) for delay‑independent stability of Lipschitz nonlinear systems.

Theorem 3.4.1 (Lyapunov–Krasovskii Delay‑Independent Stability).
Consider the delayed system

\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = f(\mathbf{x}(t)) + g(\mathbf{x}(t), \mathbf{x}(t-\tau), t),

where f and g are Lipschitz and g(\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{0}, t) = 0. If there exists a C^1 positive definite radially unbounded function V(\mathbf{x}) and a positive definite nondecreasing continuous function \psi(\cdot) such that

\frac{\partial V}{\partial \mathbf{x}} \cdot f(\mathbf{x}) \le -2\psi(V(\mathbf{x})),

\left|\frac{\partial V}{\partial \mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{a}) \cdot g(\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b}, t)\right| \le \psi(V(\mathbf{a})) + \varepsilon\psi(V(\mathbf{b})),

for some \varepsilon \in (0,1), then all trajectories are bounded and the origin is globally delay‑independently asymptotically stable.

Proof. The Lyapunov derivative along trajectories is \dot{V} = \frac{\partial V}{\partial \mathbf{x}} \cdot f + \frac{\partial V}{\partial \mathbf{x}} \cdot g \le -2\psi(V(\mathbf{x})) + \psi(V(\mathbf{x})) + \varepsilon\psi(V(\mathbf{x}(t-\tau))) = -\psi(V(\mathbf{x})) + \varepsilon\psi(V(\mathbf{x}(t-\tau))). By induction on the time evolution, one can show V is bounded and converges to zero. ∎

For our civilisation system, f captures the intra‑settlement legislative drift and instantaneous inter‑settlement couplings, while g captures the delayed couplings. The LKF approach allows us to design control gains u_i(t) = -K_i \theta_i(t) that modify f to satisfy the inequalities. The resulting LMI feasibility problem (described in detail in Chapter 2.6 of the earlier draft) can be solved for a given delay profile to yield a stabilising controller.

Theorem 3.4.2 (LMI for Delayed Civilisation Stability).
For the linearised civilisation dynamics

\dot{\delta\mathbf{X}}(t) = \mathbf{A}_0 \delta\mathbf{X}(t) + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \mathbf{A}_k \delta\mathbf{X}(t-\tau_k) + \mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}(t),

with delayed state feedback \mathbf{u}(t) = \mathbf{K} \delta\mathbf{X}(t-\tau_u), the closed‑loop system is exponentially stable if there exist matrices P>0, Q_k>0, R>0 satisfying the LMI (3.3) from the earlier draft (repeated here for completeness):

\begin{bmatrix}
\Phi & P\mathbf{A}_1 & \cdots & P\mathbf{A}_K & \tau_1 \mathbf{A}_0^T R \\
* & -Q_1 & \cdots & 0 & \tau_1 \mathbf{A}_1^T R \\
\vdots & & \ddots & & \vdots \\
* & * & & -Q_K & \tau_K \mathbf{A}_K^T R \\
* & * & & * & -R
\end{bmatrix} < 0,

with \Phi = \mathbf{A}_0^T P + P\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum Q_k + \mathbf{K}^T \mathbf{B}^T P + P\mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}.

