3.5 Decoupling via Laplacian Eigenvalues

The previous sections established stability conditions for the full vectorised system. A powerful simplification is provided by the consensus decoupling theorem (Background, Theorem 12.1), which reduces the N-agent problem to N-1 scalar problems.

Theorem 3.5.1 (Consensus Decoupling via Laplacian).
Consider the multi‑agent system with delays:

\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \sum_{d=0}^{m} (\mathbf{I}_N \otimes \mathbf{A}_d) \mathbf{x}(t-\tau_d) + \sum_{d=0}^{m} (\mathbf{L} \otimes \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}_d\mathbf{C}) \mathbf{x}(t-\tau_d - \tau_c).

Let \mathbf{U} diagonalise the Laplacian \mathbf{L}: \mathbf{U}^T \mathbf{L} \mathbf{U} = \boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(0, \lambda_2, \dots, \lambda_N). Define the transformed state \tilde{\mathbf{x}}(t) = (\mathbf{U}^T \otimes \mathbf{I}_p) \mathbf{x}(t). Then the consensus condition x_i - x_j \to 0 for all i,j is equivalent to \tilde{x}_i \to 0 for i=2,\dots,N, where each \tilde{x}_i evolves according to the independent scalar delayed system:

\dot{\tilde{\mathbf{x}}}_i(t) = \sum_{d=0}^{m} \mathbf{A}_d \tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t-\tau_d) + \lambda_i \sum_{d=0}^{m} \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}_d\mathbf{C} \tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t-\tau_d - \tau_c).
\tag{3.1}

Thus, the N-agent system is stable if and only if the N-1 decoupled systems (3.1) are simultaneously asymptotically stable.

Proof. The transformation separates the consensus mode (eigenvalue 0) from the disagreement modes. The consensus mode evolves independently and does not affect convergence to zero of the others. ∎

This theorem justifies the master stability function analysis of Chapter 2 and provides the rigorous foundation for the delay bounds derived there. Each mode i has an effective coupling strength \lambda_i, and the maximal delay depends on the largest \lambda_i, i.e., the spectral radius of \mathbf{L}. This decoupling will also be essential in Chapter 7 when designing the digital twin observer, as the observer gains can be assigned per mode.

