3.6 The Control‑Rate Governability Bound: An Information‑Theoretic Necessary Condition

The structural and Lyapunov conditions are sufficient for stability. We now turn to a necessary condition: a minimum interplanetary communication capacity below which no controller can stabilise the constitutional divergence. This is the correct formulation of the “Shannon Bound” sketched in the original proposal, grounded in the data‑rate theorem of Nair & Evans.

Theorem 3.6.1 (Control‑Rate Governability Bound).
Let the constitutional divergence dynamics be linearised as \dot{\mathbf{e}}(t) = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{e}(t) + \mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}(t) + \mathbf{w}(t), where \mathbf{e} \in \mathbb{R}^m represents the independent legislative degrees of freedom across the civilisation. Suppose the open‑loop matrix \mathbf{A} has topological entropy h(\mathbf{A}) = \sum_{i: \Re(\lambda_i)>0} \lambda_i. Then any causal feedback control law that stabilises the system in the bounded‑mean‑square‑error sense requires a communication channel (from sensors to controller, across interplanetary distances) with anytime capacity C satisfying

C \ge \frac{h(\mathbf{A})}{\ln 2} \quad \text{bits per second}.

If the available channel capacity is less than this threshold, no controller—regardless of delay or encoding sophistication—can stabilise the system.

Proof. This is the classical data‑rate theorem applied to the civilisational setting. The topological entropy quantifies the rate at which the uncontrolled system generates new uncertainty. The anytime capacity must support that rate; delay impacts the reliability exponent but not the asymptotic rate threshold (Franceschetti & Minero, 2023). ∎

Corollary 3.6.2 (Constitutional Innovation Rate).
A civilisation whose legal system generates rapid, unpredictable legislative innovations (high “legislative temperature”) demands proportionally high interplanetary bandwidth. Hence, to govern across light‑lag, one must either (a) increase channel capacity C, (b) reduce the constitutional entropy h(\mathbf{A}) by making local laws more predictable (e.g., via strong rule‑of‑law constraints), or (c) partition the civilisation into domains whose mutual channel capacities satisfy the bound.

