3.7 Smith Predictors and Predictive Governance

When delays exceed the fundamental stability limit given by the LMI or the argument principle, the system cannot be stabilised by instantaneous feedback alone. Control theory provides a classical remedy: the Smith predictor (Smith, 1957). The idea is to maintain a real‑time model of the remote settlement’s governance dynamics, synchronised via delayed measurements, and use its predictions to cancel the delay in the feedback loop.

Definition 3.7.1 (Constitutional Smith Predictor).
For each pair of settlements (i,j), node i runs an internal model \hat{\Theta}_{j|i} of node j’s legislative state, updated by the delayed measurements:

\hat{\Theta}_{j|i}(t) = \Theta_j(t-\tau_{ij}) + \int_{t-\tau_{ij}}^t f_j(\hat{\Theta}_{j|i}(s), \dots) \, ds,

where f_j is the known local dynamic of node j. The control law then uses \hat{\Theta}_{j|i}(t) instead of the raw delayed measurement.

Theorem 3.7.2 (Stabilisation via Predictive Governance).
Assume each node’s local governance dynamics are linear and perfectly modelled. Under the Smith‑predictor scheme, the closed‑loop characteristic equation no longer contains the destabilising delay terms; the delay is moved outside the feedback loop. Hence, the system can be stabilised for any finite delay \tau, provided the local models are exact and the controller gains stabilise the delay‑free system.

Proof. Standard result: the Smith predictor introduces a model of the plant delay, so the actual delay appears only in the unobservable part of the loop, not in the feedback stability equation. ∎

Practical caveats. The predictor’s performance is fragile to model mismatch. In our setting, this means that unpredictable legislative “innovations” (high constitutional entropy) degrade the prediction and reduce the effectiveness of the Smith predictor. This directly connects back to Theorem 3.6.1: to use a Smith predictor across Mars, one must either keep the innovation rate low (simple, stable legal systems) or maintain a very high‑fidelity Civilisational Digital Twin that can accurately forecast the remote constitution.

