3.8 Numerical Demonstration: Earth–Moon–Mars

We return to the three‑node, two‑layer model. Using the decoupling Theorem 3.5.1, the governance layer stability is governed by two scalar modes (since N=3, N-1=2). The Laplacian eigenvalues for symmetric coupling a_{EM}=a_{ME}=1, a_{ER}=a_{RE}=a, a_{MR}=0 are:

\lambda_2 = 1,\quad \lambda_3 = 1 + 2a.

The critical delays for each mode are \tau_2^* = \pi/2, \tau_3^* = \pi/(2(1+2a)). For a=0.5, \lambda_3 = 2.0, giving \tau_3^* \approx 0.785 in normalised time, corresponding to \approx 8.2 min when the normalisation constant is chosen to map Earth–Mars delay to the model. Thus, for the average Earth–Mars distance, the mode with the larger eigenvalue becomes unstable first.

Introducing a Smith predictor at Earth and Mars (each running a model of the other’s legislative evolution) removes the destabilising delay from both modes, provided the legislative innovation rate is low. Simulation (to be fully reported in Chapter 8) shows that with a perfect internal model, CC(t) stabilises above 0.85 even for \tau=22 min. With a 10% model mismatch (simulated by adding noise to the prediction), coherence degrades to 0.75 but remains above the critical threshold \theta_c=0.7. The required Earth–Mars data rate for the predictor correction is approximately 1 kbps under ideal coding, well within projected interplanetary link capacities.

