4.3 Category-Theoretic Foundations: The Category \mathbf{Civ} and Functorial Evolution

Before introducing connections and curvature, we embed the entire structure in a category, which will later justify the gauge‑theoretic treatment. The following theorems are drawn from the Background document (Section 8).

Definition 4.3.1 (The Category of Civilisations \mathbf{Civ}).
The category \mathbf{Civ} has:

· Objects: Civilisation states \mathcal{C} = (P, E, G, K, A, N), or equivalently the sections \sigma of the constitutional bundle together with the states of the other five layers.
· Morphisms: Civilisation transformations—evolution over a time interval, policy changes, technological transitions.
· Composition: Sequential application.
· Identity: The “do nothing” transformation.

Theorem 4.3.2 (Functoriality of Civilisation Evolution).
The evolution operator \mathcal{E}_t: \mathbf{Civ} \to \mathbf{Civ} that advances the civilisation by time t is an endofunctor, i.e., it preserves composition and identity:

\mathcal{E}_{t_1 + t_2} = \mathcal{E}_{t_1} \circ \mathcal{E}_{t_2}, \qquad \mathcal{E}_0 = \text{id}_{\mathbf{Civ}}.

Proof. The semigroup property of the DDE solutions (existence and uniqueness from Theorem 2.2.1) guarantees that time evolution defines a functor from the monoid (\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}, +, 0) to the category of endofunctors on \mathbf{Civ}. ∎

This functorial perspective means that any transformation of the civilisation—whether a natural drift or a deliberate control action—can be composed, and the result of sequential changes is independent of the intermediate state representation, a crucial property for a distributed governance system.

