4.4 Constitutional Trajectory Uniqueness and Phase Space Volume Preservation

The fiber bundle picture is physically meaningful only if constitutional trajectories are well‑behaved. Two theorems from the Background (Theorems 5.1 and 5.2 / 2.2) provide the necessary geometric guarantees.

Theorem 4.4.1 (Constitutional Trajectory Uniqueness).
If the constitutional dynamics \dot{\boldsymbol{\Theta}} = f(\boldsymbol{\Theta}) is Lipschitz continuous and autonomous, then unique constitutional trajectories cannot intersect at an angle in phase space. In particular, a single trajectory cannot intersect itself.

Proof. (From Background, Theorem 5.1). Suppose two trajectories cross at \bar{\mathbf{h}}. By Lipschitz continuity, they must have the same derivative at that point, hence they must coincide for all time by uniqueness of solutions to the initial value problem. Thus they cannot cross at an angle. ∎

This implies that the constitutional fiber bundle is a foliation: each initial condition gives rise to a unique, non‑intersecting trajectory in the bundle. The architect can therefore assign a unique future to each initial legal configuration, and the global section \sigma_t evolves deterministically.

Theorem 4.4.2 (Volume‑Preserving Constitutional Evolution).
For the linearised constitutional dynamics around a trajectory, define the density matrix \boldsymbol{\varrho} = |\delta\mathbf{u}\rangle\langle\delta\mathbf{u}|. The evolution

\frac{d}{dt}\boldsymbol{\varrho} = \{\bar{\mathbf{A}}_+, \boldsymbol{\varrho}\} + [\mathbf{A}_-, \boldsymbol{\varrho}],

where \mathbf{A}_+ and \mathbf{A}_- are the symmetric and anti‑symmetric parts of the stability matrix \mathbf{A}, preserves the phase space volume even for dissipative (non‑Hamiltonian) constitutional systems. The volume form is invariant under the tangent flow generated by the anti‑symmetric part.

Proof. (From Background, Theorem 2.2). The trace \text{Tr}(\dot{\boldsymbol{\varrho}}) = 0 follows from the commutator and anti‑commutator structure. The operator \mathbf{M}_- constructed from \mathbf{A}_- generates orthogonal transformations, preserving the Lebesgue measure in the tangent space. ∎

This result is profound: it provides an invariant measure on the constitutional phase space, which can be used to formulate ergodic‑theoretic questions about the long‑term distribution of legal systems. For the MCA architect, it means that constitutional phase space does not “shrink” over time; information is not inherently lost, only rearranged. The implications for long‑term cultural and legal diversity are significant.

