4.5 Connection, Parallel Transport, and Curvature

With the geometric foundation laid, we now introduce the constitutional connection, which formalises how legal changes propagate.

Definition 4.5.1 (Constitutional Connection 1‑Form).
For each edge (i,j), define a linear map A_{ij}: T_{\Theta_i}\mathcal{C}_i \to T_{\Theta_j}\mathcal{C}_j that takes an infinitesimal legal change at i and outputs the required infinitesimal adjustment at j to maintain compatibility. The delayed parallel transport equation is

\frac{d}{dt}\Theta_j(t+\tau_{ij}) = A_{ij}(\Theta_i(t)) \, \dot{\Theta}_i(t).
\tag{4.1}

For the Euclidean tolerance model \phi_{ij} = \exp(-\|\Theta_i - \Theta_j\|^2/2\sigma_\theta^2), the natural gradient connection is

A_{ij} = \kappa_{ij} \, \frac{1}{\sigma_\theta^2} (\Theta_i - \Theta_j),

which yields the linear delayed consensus dynamics of Chapter 2.

Definition 4.5.2 (Curvature and Holonomy).
The holonomy around a closed cycle i_1 \to i_2 \to \cdots \to i_k \to i_1 is the composition of parallel transports along the cycle, accounting for delays. The curvature F is the infinitesimal version: on a triangle (i,j,k),

F_{ijk} = A_{ij} \circ A_{jk} \circ A_{ki} - \text{id}.

If F_{ijk} = 0, the bundle is flat and a globally compatible section exists. Non‑zero curvature obstructs global coherence.

Theorem 4.5.3 (Fragmentation via Curvature).
If the constitutional bundle possesses non‑zero curvature on a cycle whose round‑trip light time is less than the characteristic event time T_{\text{event}}, then autonomous evolution under (4.1) drives CC(t) \to 0 for generic initial conditions.

Proof. Same as the earlier sketch, now underpinned by the volume‑preserving and uniqueness properties that prevent collapse to a compatible submanifold. ∎

