4.6 Sheaf-Theoretic Coherence and the Global Colimit

The bundle formulation is global. For a distributed, asynchronous system, a sheaf‑theoretic perspective is more natural: local compatibility conditions must “glue” to form a global section. The Background provides the categorical machinery (Theorem 8.2).

Definition 4.6.1 (Sheaf of Compatible Constitutions).
Let U \subseteq V be a subset of settlements. A local section over U is an assignment i \mapsto \Theta_i for i \in U such that for all edges (i,j) in U, \phi_{ij}(\Theta_i, \Theta_j) > S_{\text{threshold}}. The collection of all such local sections forms a sheaf \mathcal{F} on the discrete topological space of V.

Theorem 4.6.2 (Global Constitution as Colimit).
The global civilisation state can be constructed as the colimit of the local digital twin models:

CDT = \underset{i}{\text{colim}}\, DT_i.

In the sheaf \mathcal{F}, a global section (a coherent global constitution) exists if and only if the first sheaf cohomology group vanishes: H^1(\mathcal{B}, \mathcal{F}) = 0. Non‑zero elements of H^1 are the irreducible “legal tensions” that prevent gluing.

Proof. The colimit construction in the category of sheaves ensures that compatible local data glue to form a unique global datum. The obstruction is measured by the Čech cohomology group H^1, which is precisely the set of mismatches on triple intersections that cannot be resolved by adjusting local sections (Background, Theorem 8.2). ∎

Architect’s diagnostic. Compute H^1(\mathcal{B}, \mathcal{F}) for the current settlement graph and compatibility thresholds. If non‑zero, the architect must either add new inter‑settlement links (increase bandwidth to cut cycles), lower S_{\text{threshold}} (accept looser coupling), or formally fission the sheaf into independent domains.

