4.7 Natural Transformations as Constitutional Amendments

The category \mathbf{Civ} allows us to formalise the notion of a constitutional amendment that is compatible with the ongoing evolution of the civilisation. The Background’s Theorem 8.3 provides the key insight.

Theorem 4.7.1 (Natural Transformation as Amendment).
A constitutional amendment is a natural transformation \eta: \mathcal{F} \Rightarrow \mathcal{G} between two civilisation evolution functors \mathcal{F} and \mathcal{G}. The naturality condition requires that the following diagram commutes for any civilisation transformation f: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}':

\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{C}) & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{F}(f)} & \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{C}') \\
\eta_{\mathcal{C}} \downarrow & & \downarrow \eta_{\mathcal{C}'} \\
\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{C}) & \xrightarrow{\mathcal{G}(f)} & \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{C}')
\end{array}

This means that the amendment \eta is compatible with all possible civilisation transformations—it does not introduce inconsistencies when applied before or after other changes.

Proof. (From Background, Theorem 8.3). The naturality square ensures that applying the amendment and then evolving is equivalent to evolving and then applying the amendment. This is the precise categorical analogue of a gauge‑invariant legal reform. ∎

In the fiber bundle language, a natural transformation between functors corresponds to a gauge transformation of the connection A_{ij}. Laws that are related by such transformations represent the same physical civilisational state.

