A.2 Required Background Theorems

We recall, without proof, standard results that are used throughout the dissertation.

Theorem A.2.1 (Picard–Lindelöf for DDEs). Consider the delay differential equation \dot{x}(t) = f(t, x_t) where x_t(\theta) = x(t+\theta) for \theta \in [-\tau,0], with initial history \phi \in C([-\tau,0],\mathbb{R}^n). If f is continuous and locally Lipschitz in its second argument uniformly in t, then there exists a unique local solution. If additionally the solutions are a priori bounded, the solution is global.

Theorem A.2.2 (Lyapunov–Krasovskii Stability). For a DDE \dot{x}(t) = f(t, x_t), if there exists a continuous functional V: C([-\tau,0],\mathbb{R}^n) \to \mathbb{R} and positive constants c_1, c_2, c_3 such that c_1\|\phi(0)\|^2 \le V(\phi) \le c_2\|\phi\|_\tau^2 and \dot{V}(\phi) \le -c_3\|\phi(0)\|^2, then the zero solution is uniformly asymptotically stable.

Theorem A.2.3 (Data Processing Inequality). If X \to Y \to Z is a Markov chain, then I(X;Z) \le I(X;Y).

Theorem A.2.4 (Data-Rate Theorem). Consider the linear system \dot{x} = Ax + Bu + w with A having eigenvalues in the open right half-plane. For any causal feedback law that stabilises the system in the sense of bounded state variance, the feedback channel must have an anytime capacity C \ge \sum_{\Re(\lambda_i)>0} \lambda_i / \ln 2.

