B.1 Proof of Theorem 2.2.1 (Existence and Uniqueness of Civilisation Trajectories)

Statement. Assume each G_\alpha^{(i)} and C_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)} is locally Lipschitz continuous and bounded on compact sets, and the state space is compact. Then for any initial history \mathbf{X}_0 \in C([-\tau_{\max},0], \mathbb{R}^D), there exists a unique global solution to (2.2) for all t \ge 0.

Proof. The right-hand side of (2.2) defines a functional F: C([-\tau_{\max},0], \mathbb{R}^D) \to \mathbb{R}^D given by

F(\mathbf{X}_t) = \left[G_\alpha^{(i)}(x_\alpha^{(i)}(0)) + \sum_{\beta, j} C_{\alpha\beta}^{(ij)}\big(x_\alpha^{(i)}(0), x_\beta^{(j)}(-\tau_{ij})\big) + \Xi_\alpha^{(i)}(t)\right]_{\alpha,i}.

By hypothesis, G and C are locally Lipschitz in their arguments, and the state space is a subset of a compact set (e.g., a product of bounded intervals for each coordinate). Therefore F is locally Lipschitz in the history segment \mathbf{X}_t, uniformly in t. By Theorem A.2.1, a unique local solution exists. Because the state variables are bounded by the compactness assumption, the solution can be extended indefinitely, yielding global existence and uniqueness. ∎

