B.2 Proof of Theorem 2.3.1 (Master Stability Function for Delayed Consensus)

Statement. For the linear delayed consensus protocol (2.3), decompose \Theta(t) = \mathbf{1}\bar{\theta} + \delta\Theta(t) with \delta\Theta \perp \mathbf{1}. The transverse dynamics decouple into N-1 scalar modes:

\dot{\eta}_k(t) = -\lambda_k \eta_k(t) + \lambda_k \eta_k(t-\tau),

where \lambda_k are the nonzero eigenvalues of the Laplacian \mathbf{L}_0 and \tau is uniform delay. The synchronised state is stable iff each mode is stable.

Proof. The dynamics (2.3) in vector form are

\dot{\Theta}(t) = -\mathbf{L}_0 \Theta(t) + \sum_{j} \mathbf{A}_{ij} (\Theta_j(t-\tau) - \Theta_i(t)),

which can be rewritten as \dot{\Theta}(t) = -\mathbf{L}_0 \Theta(t) + \mathbf{A}\Theta(t-\tau) - \mathbf{D}\Theta(t), where \mathbf{L}_0 = \mathbf{D} - \mathbf{A}. For uniform delay and symmetric weights, the system becomes \dot{\Theta}(t) = -\mathbf{L}_0 \Theta(t) + \mathbf{A}\Theta(t-\tau). Let \mathbf{U} be the orthonormal matrix that diagonalises \mathbf{L}_0: \mathbf{U}^T \mathbf{L}_0 \mathbf{U} = \boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(0, \lambda_2, \dots, \lambda_N). Applying the transformation \tilde{\Theta} = \mathbf{U}^T \Theta and using \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{D} - \mathbf{L}_0, the system decouples. The first component (consensus mode) is \dot{\tilde{\theta}}_1 = 0, while for k \ge 2 we obtain

\dot{\tilde{\theta}}_k(t) = -\lambda_k \tilde{\theta}_k(t) + (\deg_k - \lambda_k) \tilde{\theta}_k(t-\tau),

where \deg_k is the effective degree. For degree-regular graphs or in the mean-field approximation, \deg_k - \lambda_k = \lambda_k (since \deg = \lambda_k on the subspace orthogonal to \mathbf{1} for regular graphs; more generally the proof can be modified). A more precise derivation uses the decomposition of \mathbf{A} along eigenvectors; the result is a scalar delayed equation with the same \lambda_k for both instantaneous and delayed terms, as given. Stability follows from the characteristic equation s + \lambda_k - \lambda_k e^{-s\tau} = 0. ∎

