B.3 Proof of Theorem 3.2.1 (Delay-Independent Consensus)

Statement. If the switching digraph is uniformly jointly strongly connected, then the consensus protocol with arbitrary bounded time-varying delays achieves asymptotic consensus.

Proof. (Sketch, based on contraction theory). Consider the Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{x}) = \max_i x_i - \min_i x_i, the diameter of the state vector. Under the delayed consensus protocol, the contraction rate of V can be bounded by a quantity that depends on the joint connectivity of the graph over intervals of length T. By uniform joint strong connectivity, there exists \delta > 0 such that over any interval of length T, the union of active edges forms a strongly connected graph. The delay does not prevent information propagation because the protocol still drives states towards each other whenever a delayed edge is active; the diameter strictly decreases over each T-interval. Standard arguments (Moreau, 2005) guarantee convergence to consensus. ∎

