B.4 Proof of Theorem 3.3.1 (Delay-Dependent Robust Stability via Argument Principle)

Statement. For the uncertain system \dot{\boldsymbol{\eta}} = \sum \mathbf{A}_d \boldsymbol{\eta}(t-\tau_d) + (\alpha + \Delta\alpha) \sum \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}_d\mathbf{C} \boldsymbol{\eta}(t-\tau_d - \tau_c), stability is guaranteed if

\sup_{s \in i\mathbb{R}} \|\mathbf{R}^{-1}(s)\| \sum_d \|\mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}_d\mathbf{C}\| < \frac{1}{\Delta\alpha}.

Proof. The characteristic equation is \det(\mathbf{R}(s) + \Delta\alpha \sum \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}_d\mathbf{C} e^{-(\tau_d+\tau_c)s}) = 0. For the nominal system (\Delta\alpha=0), stability is assumed. The perturbation is bounded. By the argument principle, the number of unstable poles does not change if \|\Delta\alpha \sum \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}_d\mathbf{C} e^{-(\tau_d+\tau_c)s}\| < \|\mathbf{R}^{-1}(s)\|^{-1} for all s on the Nyquist contour. The supremum condition ensures this. ∎

