B.8 Proof of Theorem 4.7.1 (Natural Transformation as Constitutional Amendment)

Statement. A constitutional amendment is a natural transformation \eta: \mathcal{F} \Rightarrow \mathcal{G} between evolution functors.

Proof. Consider two possible evolution functors \mathcal{F} and \mathcal{G} that differ by a change in the governing equations (e.g., a new treaty). A family of maps \eta_{\mathcal{C}}: \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{C}) \to \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{C}) is natural if for every civilisation transformation f: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}', the square commutes. This exactly means that applying the amendment first and then evolving under the new law (\mathcal{G}(f) \circ \eta_{\mathcal{C}}) yields the same result as evolving under the old law and then amending (\eta_{\mathcal{C}'} \circ \mathcal{F}(f)). This is the categorical formulation of a policy that is consistent across time and space. ∎

